{"The game in the Open Tournev in which Mrs Holloway (Bromley) won against Mr
R. E. Lean (Brighton) was of interest as an example of the standard of play
that ladies are able to achieve. The moves went on to a total of about 70, but
the following shows the essential portion of the game, as what followed was
really manoeuvring for posi­tion, in which Mrs Holloway improved upon her
advantage. The moves that really settled the contest were:-"} 1. e4 e5 2. d4
Nc6 3. Nf3 exd4 4. Bc4 Bc5 5. O-O d6 6. c3 Bg4 7. Qb3 Bxf3 8. Bxf7+ Kf8 9. gxf3
Ne5 10. Bh5 Qh4 11. cxd4 Bxd4 12. f4 Ng6 13. Bxg6 hxg6 14. Qg3 Qh5 15. Nc3 Nf6
16. Re1 Ng4 17. Be3 Bxe3 18. fxe3 Nxh2 19. Kg2 Qg4 20. Rh1 {... 70 moves.
Source: Western Daily Press, 9 April 1926} 1-0

